.TH BIGFLOATADD 3 "2026-09-21" "BigNums" "Library Functions Manual"
.nr HY 0
.ds AD l
.SH NAME
bigFloatAdd, bigFloatSub, bigFloatMul \- perform signed big float arithmetic
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B #include \(dqbignums.h\(dq
.sp
.BI "int bigFloatAdd(BigFloat *" "result" ", const BigFloat *" "a" ","
.BI "    const BigFloat *" "b" ");"
.sp
.BI "int bigFloatSub(BigFloat *" "result" ", const BigFloat *" "a" ","
.BI "    const BigFloat *" "b" ");"
.sp
.BI "int bigFloatMul(BigFloat *" "result" ", const BigFloat *" "a" ","
.BI "    const BigFloat *" "b" ");"
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
These functions store a plus b, a minus b, or a times b in result.
Use a distinct output object; there is no general aliasing contract for all
floating-point operations.  Inputs must be initialized; a distinct output
need not be.
.PP
Addition aligns both mantissas to the larger exponent by right-shifting the
other mantissa.  Low bits may be discarded.  Equal signs select addition;
different signs select magnitude comparison and unsigned subtraction.
Subtraction negates a copy of b and calls addition.
.PP
Multiplication uses FFT mantissa multiplication, combines signs and exponents,
and normalizes the product.  It checks the exponent sum using a wider integer.
There is no target precision argument or automatic product truncation.
.SH RETURN VALUE
Addition and subtraction return 0 on success, \-1 on a delegated shift or
subtraction error, or INT_MAX on capacity/normalization failure.
Multiplication returns 0 or INT_MAX; even an FFT allocation failure is mapped
to INT_MAX.  Outputs may have been modified on failure.
.SH BUGS
Addition can lose low-order information when aligning exponents.
Not all exponent arithmetic, including alignment differences and final
normalization, is checked for signed overflow.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR bigFloatCmpAbs (3),
.BR bigIntMulFFT (3),
.BR bigFloatTruncate (3),
.BR bignums (3),
.BR bignums (7)
